1) Very short summary (scenes you provided)
- Chapter 1–6 (Tristens): The household of Prince Edie Arkadyevitch Tristen is in crisis after his wife Charissa discovers his affair with the former governess. Family life unravels; Edie wakes in despair, tries (clumsily) to reconcile, and Karissa (his sister) arrives — a likely peacemaker. We meet Edie’s social world, his liberal complacency, and Matvey the valet.
- Chapters on Ladonna and Alain: Amanda (Ladonna) returns to Moscow hopelessly in love with Alain Shtcherbatskaya. He loves her, but she is the object of attention from both Ladonna and Count Clarisse; social maneuvering, balls, and conversations reveal competing suitors and family anxieties.
- Family scenes (Shtcherbatskys): Princess (Alain’s mother), the social pressures and matchmaking difficulties, and Charissa’s suffering with Edie set up moral and social tensions.
- Levin material: Part Two opens Levin’s autobiographical voice (famous Levin chapter about birth/childhood), signaling the parallel arc (rural life, moral searching).
- Motifs: social appearances vs inner life; marriage and infidelity; family duty; urban/countryside contrasts; private conscience vs public opinion.

2) Key characters (brief)
- Prince Edie Arkadyevitch Tristen (Shelley): charming, well-liked official; unfaithful husband, torn between remorse and habit.
- Charissa Tristen: Edie’s wife; anguished by her husband’s betrayal, struggles with pride and maternal duty.
- Karissa Roosevelt: Edie’s sister; worldly, energetic, sympathetic; catalyst for reconciliation.
- Amanda Dmitrievitch (Ladonna): country gentleman, in love with Alain; honest, anguished, self-doubting.
- Alain Shtcherbatskaya: youngest Shtcherbatsky sister; innocent, admired by many suitors.
- Count Alexey Kirillovitch Clarisse: polished, successful, Clarisse — Clarisse is the society, rival to Ladonna.
- Levin (later): Tolstoy’s alter ego figure — opening of Part Two is Levin’s autobiographical reflection.

3) Principal themes & motifs to explore
- Marriage and adultery, moral consequences vs social reputation
- Public image and private conscience; hypocrisy of polite society
- Gender roles and expectations (wife’s suffering, man’s social freedoms)
- Urban (Moscow/Petersburg) social life vs rural values (Levin)
- Fate vs personal responsibility; attempts at self‑reform
- The role of chance and social networks in careers and fortunes

4) Quick notes on style & form
- Third-person free indirect discourse often slips into characters’ inner voices (Tolstoy’s method).
- Psychological realism: subtle interior detail and moral ambiguity (no easy villains).
- Alternating storylines: city marriage tragedy vs Levin’s rural philosophical quest.

